---
import ChronicleLayout from '../../layouts/ChronicleLayout.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../../components/chronicle/ArticleHeader.astro';
import AnchorNav from '../../components/chronicle/AnchorNav.astro';
import Chapter from '../../components/chronicle/Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/chronicle/Figure.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/chronicle/SectionHeading.astro';
import AtAGlance from '../../components/chronicle/AtAGlance.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/chronicle/FurtherReading.astro';
import type { ChronicleMeta } from '../../components/chronicle/types';
import { SIZES_MODERN_WIDE } from '../../lib/responsive-image-config.mjs';

const meta: ChronicleMeta = {
  title: 'Best Things to Do in Mendoza: A First-Hand Guide',
  headline: 'Best Things to Do in Mendoza',
  description:
    'A first-hand guide to the best things to do in Mendoza, from Parque General San Martín and Cerro de la Gloria to the city itself and a day in wine country.',
  path: '/best-things-to-do-in-mendoza/',
  ogImage: '/images/3-days-in-mendoza-itinerary/mendoza-tiled-park-1200w.jpg',
  photography: true,
};

const IMG = '/images/3-days-in-mendoza-itinerary';
---

<ChronicleLayout meta={meta}>
  <ArticleHeader
    provenance={meta}
    route={['Argentina', 'Mendoza', 'South America']}
    label="Guide · Five Experiences"
    title="Best Things to Do in Mendoza"
    standfirst="Mendoza is internationally associated with wine, but the recorded visit had another structure too: a city of parks and formal streets, a monument at the foothill edge, and a separate day assigned to wine country. These are the five experiences I would prioritise on a first visit."
    stats={[
      { num: '5', label: 'Experiences' },
      { num: '3', label: 'Nights' },
      { num: '2', label: 'Registers' },
      { num: '1', label: 'City' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          Mendoza arrived between two long transfers: after the bus from Santiago and
          before the unresolved onward journey towards Iguazú. It would have been easy
          to treat the city as a base for vineyards. The route did something more
          useful. It moved through parks, formal streets, architecture and monuments
          before giving a separate day to the wine-country names.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is the broad discovery guide above the <a href="/3-days-in-mendoza-itinerary/">three-day Mendoza itinerary</a>.
          It answers what deserves time rather than how the days were arranged. The
          recommendations come from the recorded stay from 23 to 26 September 2026,
          with the city and wine-country material kept distinct.
        </p>
        <p>
          The wine section is deliberately restrained. Carmelo Patti, Bodega Viamonte,
          Cecchin, Pasrai, Vistandes and Bodega Esencia 1870 appear in the route record,
          but the archive does not preserve tasting notes, favourite producers, prices,
          operators or reliably matched vineyard photographs. This is not a winery
          ranking.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/mendoza-tiled-park.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/mendoza-tiled-park-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/mendoza-tiled-park-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/mendoza-tiled-park-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/mendoza-tiled-park-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/mendoza-tiled-park.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A tiled public park in Mendoza with a central monument and mature trees."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Mendoza begins here as a city of formal greenery, not only as a route to the vineyards."
      priority
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="how-to-use" rule={false}>How to use this list</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The order moves from the city towards the foothill edge, then out to the
        recorded wine-country day. It is not a universal ranking. The city material is
        photographically and personally stronger; the wine material is important to
        the shape of the stay but remains a bounded route record.
      </p>
      <p>
        Route details below are not current opening hours, ticket prices, transport
        fares or winery advice. The evidence does not preserve those things, so they
        belong to separate pre-trip checking rather than to this first-hand guide.
      </p>
    </div>

    <AnchorNav
      links={[
        { id: 'central', label: 'Central Mendoza' },
        { id: 'san-martin', label: 'Parque San Martín' },
        { id: 'gloria', label: 'Cerro de la Gloria' },
        { id: 'san-vicente', label: 'San Vicente' },
        { id: 'wine-country', label: 'Wine country' },
        { id: 'priorities', label: 'Priorities' },
      ]}
    />
  </div>

  <AtAGlance
    id="glance"
    title="The Five Experiences at a Glance"
    meta={[
      {
        label: 'Start in the city',
        value: 'Central Mendoza and Parque General San Martín establish the urban side of the visit before the route moves outwards.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Add the foothill edge',
        value: 'Cerro de la Gloria and San Vicente Park widen the city route without turning it into an unsupported hiking guide.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Keep wine separate',
        value: 'The recorded wine-country day belongs in the overview, but only as a route-level experience rather than a tasting review.',
      },
    ]}
    daysHeading="Choose by interest"
    days={[
      { num: 'City', text: 'Central streets · architecture · Mendoza University · urban movement' },
      { num: 'Green', text: 'Parque General San Martín · formal landscaping · monuments' },
      { num: 'Edge', text: 'Cerro de la Gloria · San Vicente Park · city-to-foothill geography' },
      { num: 'Wine', text: 'A separate recorded day among named wine-country stops' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <Chapter id="central" number="I." name="Explore central Mendoza" epithet="Tree-lined streets, architecture and the city between the larger stops" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Central Mendoza is the first thing I would retain from the visit. The route
        moved through tree-lined streets, formal buildings, monuments and ordinary city
        movement before the parks and foothill material took over. Mendoza University
        also appears in this city sequence, but only as a recorded area stop: there is
        no campus tour or institutional visit to describe.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the part of Mendoza that disappears when the city is treated only as a
        transfer point for wineries. The evidence does not support a measured walking
        circuit, architecture history or current café guide. It does support spending
        time with the city itself, allowing the parks and monuments to be connected by
        the streets between them.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/mendoza-street-corner.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/mendoza-street-corner-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/mendoza-street-corner-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/mendoza-street-corner-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/mendoza-street-corner-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/mendoza-street-corner.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A Mendoza street corner from the recorded city route."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="A Mendoza street corner from the city route, between parks, buildings and monuments."
    />
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/mendoza-historic-facade.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/mendoza-historic-facade-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/mendoza-historic-facade-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/mendoza-historic-facade-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/mendoza-historic-facade-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/mendoza-historic-facade.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="An ornate historic facade in Mendoza with the words Banco Hipotecario Nacional."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="An identifiable historic facade in Mendoza, separate from the unillustrated wine-country stops."
    />

    <Chapter id="san-martin" number="II." name="Spend time in Parque General San Martín" epithet="The clearest expression of Mendoza as a city of parks" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Parque General San Martín is the strongest city recommendation. The recorded
        material supports greenery, formal landscaping, tree-lined routes and monument
        imagery. More importantly, the park gives Mendoza room: it changes the city
        from a sequence of streets into a wider public landscape.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have kept the description at that level. The archive does not establish a
        complete walking loop, bike rental, a particular lake or garden, café stops,
        opening arrangements or a measured amount of time. The park earns its place
        because of its scale and its relationship to the rest of the city route, not
        because a generic park checklist can be imported into the article.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/mendoza-park-monument.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/mendoza-park-monument-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/mendoza-park-monument-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/mendoza-park-monument-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/mendoza-park-monument.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="An equestrian monument on a tree-lined Mendoza park walkway."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="An equestrian monument on a tree-lined Mendoza walkway."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <Chapter id="gloria" number="III." name="Visit Cerro de la Gloria" epithet="A monument and foothill setting at the edge of the city route" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Cerro de la Gloria is where the Mendoza route changes geographical scale. The
        visit is supported as a confirmed foothill landmark and monument stop, alongside
        San Vicente Park. It gives the city a relationship with the land beyond its
        formal streets without requiring a high-country excursion.
      </p>
      <p>
        The evidence does not preserve the transport method, walking distance, climb
        difficulty, elevation, ticketing, opening hours or a reliable personal account
        of the view. I therefore describe Cerro de la Gloria as a monument in a
        foothill setting, not as a hike or viewpoint guide. Aconcagua, Potrerillos and
        the High Andes do not belong here: they were not completed Mendoza experiences
        in the supplied record.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/mendoza-modernist-tower.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/mendoza-modernist-tower-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/mendoza-modernist-tower-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/mendoza-modernist-tower-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/mendoza-modernist-tower.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A modernist Mendoza building from the city route; exact attraction is not claimed."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Modern Mendoza architecture from the wider city-and-foothill route; exact attraction not claimed."
    />

    <Chapter id="san-vicente" number="IV." name="Follow the San Vicente and city-to-foothill route" epithet="A supporting park stop that makes the city’s edge visible" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        San Vicente Park is recorded in the same broad city-and-foothill material as
        Cerro de la Gloria. I would not force it into a standalone attraction article:
        the evidence supports the stop and its place in the route, but not a detailed
        park guide, facility list, event account or route through the grounds.
      </p>
      <p>
        It works here because the combination matters. Mendoza was not only central
        streets followed by vineyards; the route also moved through parks, monuments
        and the edge of the built city. San Vicente is a supporting part of that
        experience, best chosen when the purpose is to understand the wider shape of
        Mendoza rather than to collect another named attraction.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="wine-country" number="V." name="Spend a day in Mendoza wine country" epithet="A central part of the stay, described as a route rather than a tasting review" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Wine country belongs in a first-visit overview because it occupied a separate
        recorded day. The route names Carmelo Patti, Bodega Viamonte, Cecchin, Pasrai,
        Vistandes and Bodega Esencia 1870. Those names establish the day’s wine-country
        shape and its importance within the three-night stay.
      </p>
      <p>
        They do not establish which wines were tasted, what was eaten, which stop was a
        tour rather than an exterior visit, how the route was operated, what anything
        cost or which producer was a personal favourite. The processed Mendoza images
        are city, park and architecture families; none is used as a winery or vineyard
        photograph. This section is therefore text-led and deliberately modest.
      </p>
      <p>
        The useful first-hand conclusion is about balance. Mendoza can be planned as a
        city-and-wine stay: the parks and central route deserve their own time, while
        wine country remains a separate day rather than swallowing the whole visit.
        For the dated arrangement of those stops, continue to the <a href="/3-days-in-mendoza-itinerary/">three-day itinerary</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SectionHeading id="priorities">What I Would Prioritise</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        <strong>With one day:</strong> I would choose central Mendoza and Parque
        General San Martín, then add Cerro de la Gloria only if the route and confirmed
        access allow. That combination explains the city before the wine-country day
        enters the picture.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>With two days:</strong> keep one day for the city, parks and foothill
        edge, then use the second for the recorded wine-country route. San Vicente Park
        fits as supporting geography rather than a separate full-day attraction.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>With three days:</strong> use the <a href="/3-days-in-mendoza-itinerary/">complete Mendoza itinerary</a>
        for the route chronology. This guide is the prioritisation layer above it, not
        a version with the dates removed.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SectionHeading id="surprised">What changed the way I saw Mendoza</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The city became more than a gateway once the parks and monuments were allowed
        to stand on their own. Mendoza’s wine reputation is accurate as a description
        of one part of the stay, but incomplete as a description of the city I
        recorded: formal greenery, central streets, architecture and a foothill edge
        came first, with the named wine-country day held apart.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the reason to choose Mendoza carefully. The strongest visit is not a
        competition between the city and the vineyards. It is a recognition that they
        are different experiences, and that three nights can give each of them a place
        without pretending the archive contains a complete review of either one.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SectionHeading id="omitted">What I Have Left Out</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        I have not added Aconcagua, Potrerillos, Cacheuta, Uspallata, rafting, horse
        riding, cycling tours, olive-oil tours or cooking classes. They may be familiar
        Mendoza search topics, but they are not completed experiences in this record.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have also left out wine rankings, tasting notes, restaurant reviews, tour
        operators, transport fares, current prices, opening hours, weather and a
        completed running account. The article is strongest when it helps choose what
        deserves time without pretending the route contains more detail than it does.
      </p>
    </div>

    <FurtherReading
      label="Continue through Mendoza"
      cards={[
        { href: '/3-days-in-mendoza-itinerary/', region: 'Argentina · Mendoza', title: '3 Days in Mendoza: Complete Itinerary', excerpt: 'The full route through the city, parks, foothill landmarks and the recorded wine-country day.', meta: ['Itinerary', 'September 2026'] },
        { href: '/mendoza-beyond-wine/', region: 'Argentina · Mendoza', title: 'Mendoza Beyond Wine: Exploring the City Beyond the Wineries', excerpt: 'A focused account of the parks, streets, monuments and foothill edge that make Mendoza more than a wine base.', meta: ['City guide', 'September 2026'] },
        { href: '/24-days-in-south-america-itinerary/', region: 'South America · Argentina', title: '24 Days in South America', excerpt: 'The wider route from Santiago through Mendoza and onward towards Iguazú.', meta: ['Pillar itinerary', '2026'] },
      ]}
    />
  </div>
</ChronicleLayout>
